The code is a NEAR smart contract (not Solana). From the provided CWE candidate list, the main security-relevant root causes present are resource-amplification and unbounded resource allocation patterns stemming from:
(1) the contract attaching deposits on cross-contract calls from its own balance (not the caller’s), enabling an attacker to asymmetrically drain contract funds with repeated calls (CWE-405);
(2) an async state-machine gap where `withdraw()` removes an entry before the external transfer completes, and the failure callback can re-insert without re-checking `MAX_LOCK_NUM`, allowing the per-account lock limit to be exceeded via concurrent calls (CWE-770);
(3) “optimistic” state updates that rely on `after_token_transfer` executing; if the callback does not run (insufficient gas, callback failure, upgrade/race), state may remain in the “withdrawn” state even though the transfer did not happen (prematurely releasing the lock bookkeeping) (CWE-826).
